<h2>Prepare one replacement brief</h2>
<p>For each live job record the owner, trigger, eligible audience, channel permission, offer, approval state, queued messages and stop condition. Include manual campaigns and support replies as well as automations. Mark what stays on the current system and what must be rebuilt. Export source permission evidence and current suppression separately from the marketable audience; imported addresses alone are not permission.</p>
<p>Use a proposed cart exercise: enter with an available SKU, purchase while waiting, then replay the original event. The replacement should cancel recovery after purchase and avoid duplicate entry. For a launch, change the SKU inventory after approval and require review of stale creative before dispatch. For replenishment, distinguish a gift purchase from a repeat-use order instead of assuming the same schedule fits both.</p>
<h2>Compare the same workload and cut over by readiness</h2>
<p>Quote normal and peak months with the same contacts, email volume, destination countries, SMS segments, carrier charges, numbers and labor. Count template rebuilding and managed-service replacement. Proposed fixed-cost exercise: a $100 basket discounted by 25% produces $75 revenue; subtract $40 goods and $15 other variable costs to leave $20 before messaging and platform fees. With $65 goods it leaves minus $5. Discount percentage alone cannot tell you whether an order loses money; attributed orders do not establish incremental lift.</p>
<p>Assign one active sender per purpose and channel. Cancel or explicitly transfer the old queue, reconcile fresh withdrawals and purchases, and check permission again at dispatch. Test recipient-local scheduling and unknown-timezone handling rather than imposing a universal overlap or 24-hour rule. Give support a reply owner and rollback instructions. A rollback restores the intended sender only after checking which messages already went out.</p>
<h2>Choose the next research step</h2>
<p>Use comparisons when you have two candidates; use store-type and use-case guides to define the acceptance workload. The app directory provides catalog context rather than proving a winner for your store.</p>
